Magnetism
- Drone uses strong magnetic material to coerce debris to desired containment
- Not too expensive (Neodymium)
- Not too hard to integrate
- CON: Only a small percentage of space debris is actually magnetic, given Al, Al2O3, Ti, Fe, Cu, and Ag, only one of those is magnetic

Net Deployment
- Deploy net (w/ counter force to keep stable) to "grab" debris
- can reel in material to compartment on drone, or just drag along
- Net material must be able to withstand space conditions
- CON: joey said this would be difficult

Robot Arm(s)
- Mechanically and Physcially intensive
- kinematics(forward, and inverse), Jacobian matrix
- Mechanical components: Base and arm structre, joints, actuator, end-effector
- Feedback control
- Collision Avoidance
- Algorithms and programming
- Could build and program physcial model of arm
- Personally think this would be most fun
- CON: Probably more time intensive and will require more work

Harpoon/Tether
- Long range projectile
- Pierces debris and reels it in
- Requires propulsion system to fire projectile (compressed air? gas fuel?)
- Also means it needs a couteractive force to keep steady
- CON: could shatter debris(i assume you know why thats bad), and could miss the debris entirely(also bad)

Gecko Adhesives
- Van der Waals forces
- Microscopic hairs create atomic-level suction that can be "turned on and off" when angling pad a certain way
- Low level of force required
- Controlled contact
- Better for smooth debris
- CON: probably out of our depth for this one

Ion Beam / Gas Shepherding
- Ion beam "blows" onto objects to move them out of orbit
- Avoids grappling with tumbling debris
- CON: Expensive and inefficient, and maybe too complicated

- Ambient gas alternative
  - uses thin, ambient, atmospheric gases in LEO as propellant instead of lugging around heavy fuel tanks
  - Intercepts natural gas, ionizes it using electron cloud in ionization chamber, and accelerates it towards the object using an electrostatic grid
  - Mission liespan is as long as the satellites(drones) components (meaning it doesnt rely on fuel), making it able to move between many different targets
- CON: again, probably too complicated

Inflatable Funnel
- Lightweight, expandable cone/funnel for debris to drift into
- Must calculate for a controlled capture
- CON: because, it is prone to punctures and has a general durability concern

Electrostatic Attraction
- Can generate and maintain strong electric charges in space (no air to mess it up)
- Charge drone to high coltage to create electric field to attract conductive debris w/ opposite charge or induced charges in nuetrally charged pieces
- How it works:
  - drone uses electron gun/ion emitter to charge itself
  - Nearby debris w/ natrual charge from space plasma effects or drone's field induces charge seperation
  - Electrostatic force pulls objects w/in grabbing or collecting disatance
  - Debris "sticks" to charged surface through electrostatic adhesion
- Works on conductive metals (most debris)
- Contactless attraction, safer when thinking about collisions
- No propellant needed
- Can attract multiple things

- Things to consider (not CONs but WORRYs):
  - Power management
  - Charge management
  - Non-conductive materials (plastics and other composites)
  - Space plasma effects (like solar wind) can degrade electric fields
  - Discharge risks when retrieving things to drone
